---
layout: article
title: Videos
description: Stream video and audio with Appwrite Videos. Turn files in Storage into adaptive HLS, DASH, and CMAF streams with encoding profiles, captions, and scrubbing thumbnails.
---

Appwrite **Videos** turns video and audio files in [Storage](/docs/products/storage) into adaptive streams. You create a video from a file that is already in a bucket, encode it into a ladder of renditions, and hand the master manifest URL to any HLS or DASH player.

The source file stays in its bucket. Videos never replaces it, and playback follows the same [permissions](/docs/products/videos/permissions) as the file.

You manage videos under **Videos** in your project, and encoding profiles under **Videos** > **Encoding profiles**.

# Why stream instead of serving the file {% #why-stream %}

You could put an MP4 in Storage and point a `<video>` tag at it. That works for short clips, but the viewer downloads one large file at one quality, whatever their connection. Streaming fixes this:

- **Faster start**: The player fetches a small manifest and a few seconds of video, not the whole file.
- **Fewer stalls**: The player drops to a lower quality when the connection slows, instead of pausing to buffer.
- **Better quality where possible**: On a fast connection, viewers get your sharpest rendition.
- **Less wasted bandwidth**: Viewers who stop after a minute only downloaded a minute.

Use Videos for anything longer than a short loop, or anything watched on mobile networks: courses, product demos, user uploads, recorded talks, and podcasts.

# How it works {% #how-it-works %}

A video is built from a few resources:

1. **Video**: Points at a source file in a Storage bucket and holds the media metadata probed from it, such as duration, resolution, and codecs. See [Videos](/docs/products/videos/videos).
2. **Profile**: A reusable encoding target with a resolution, video and audio bitrate, and codec. Every project starts with a default ladder of profiles. See [Encoding profiles](/docs/products/videos/profiles).
3. **Rendition**: One encode of a video against one profile, packaged for one output. Players switch between renditions as bandwidth changes. See [Renditions](/docs/products/videos/renditions).
4. **Output**: The streaming format a rendition is packaged in: **HLS**, **DASH**, or **CMAF**. Each output has a master manifest that players load first. See [Outputs](/docs/products/videos/outputs).
5. **Captions and timeline**: Text tracks listed in the manifest, and scrubbing thumbnails with a poster image. See [Captions](/docs/products/videos/captions) and [Timeline](/docs/products/videos/timeline).

```text
Storage file ──> Video ──> Renditions (profile × output) ──> Master manifest ──> Player
                   ├──> Captions
                   └──> Timeline (sprites + WebVTT)
```

# The API at a glance {% #api-at-a-glance %}

Everything the Console does is available from a [server SDK](/docs/sdks#server) with an API key. A typical integration makes four calls:

| Step | Call | Notes |
|------|------|-------|
| 1. Upload the source | `storage.createFile()` | Any bucket. Set [permissions](/docs/products/videos/permissions) here, since playback follows them |
| 2. Create the video | `videos.create({ bucketId, fileId })` | Queues the timeline. Metadata fills in shortly after |
| 3. Encode | `videos.createRendition({ videoId, profileId, output })` | Once per profile. Encoding runs in the background |
| 4. Play | Master manifest URL | Build it from the video ID. See [Outputs](/docs/products/videos/outputs#master-manifest-urls) |

To run steps 2 and 3 for every upload automatically, see [Automate with Functions](/docs/products/videos/videos#automate-with-functions).

